\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Tool Use and External Computation in LLMs.}
A growing body of work augments language models with external tools \citep{tool-learning-survey-2024}, spanning several functional categories.
Self-supervised methods learn tool invocation from loss signals \citep{toolformer-2023,toolace-2025}, token-level methods embed tools in the vocabulary \citep{toolkengpt-2023}, task-level decomposition routes subtasks to specialized modules \citep{art-2023,chameleon-2023}, and RL-based methods learn routing from reward \citep{retool-2025}.
All focus on \emph{learning to use tools}; we ask a prior question: \emph{what signal drives the routing decision?}
Concurrent work examines \emph{when} to invoke tools: tool-call decisions correlate with uncertainty~\citep{tool-overuse-illusion-2026}, are encoded in hidden states~\citep{when2tool-2026}, impose a ``tool-use tax''~\citep{tool-use-tax-2026}, or follow necessity-utility-affordability logic~\citep{to-call-or-not-2026}. Our decomposition yields a concrete answer: 78.9\% of non-tie routing labels trace to catastrophic-failure prevention.

\paragraph{Adaptive Computation and Routing.}
Adaptive routing traces back to learned halting policies \citep{graves2016act}. Mixture-of-Recursions \citep{mixture-of-recursions-2025} learns token-level recursion depths in the LLM era; concurrent work further diversifies routing granularity: CogRouter \citep{cogrouter-2026} adapts per-step depth, ARES \citep{ares-2026} selects effort levels, and MTRouter \citep{mtrouter-2026} addresses multi-turn settings; in death-dominated environments, the task-type-level binary signal is structurally inaccessible to these step-level approaches (\S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}).
RouteLLM \citep{routellm-2024} learns routers from preference data to select between strong and weak models; cost-optimization routing (FrugalGPT~\citep{frugalgpt-2023}; AutoMix~\citep{automix-2024}) cascades queries across model tiers. These methods optimize \emph{which} model to invoke; our framework diagnoses \emph{what} drives the routing decision.
Concurrently, \citet{budget-aware-routing-2026} train episode-level routing policies under sparse terminal rewards, the closest setting to ours.
Unlike token-level adaptive computation (Mixture-of-Depths~\citep{mixture-of-depths-2024}; speculative decoding~\citep{leviathan2023speculative}; early exit~\citep{xin2020deebert,zhou2020bert}) and Mixture-of-Experts architectures~\citep{switch-transformer-2022,gshard-2021}, our framework operates at episode-level granularity, diagnosing aggregate routing signal composition rather than optimizing per-token compute allocation. Counterfactual data augmentation~\citep{kaushik2020learning} edits inputs to flip labels; our framework executes full counterfactual \emph{episodes} to decompose routing signal at the environment level.

\paragraph{Counterfactual Evaluation in RL.}
Prior work generates counterfactual explanations by perturbing individual actions \citep{olson2021counterfactual, gajcin2023counterfactual, amitai2024explaining}; recent and concurrent extensions target LLM agents~\citep{pona2025abstract,axis-2026,cso-2026}. We instead execute complete episodes under two strategies to decompose routing signal \emph{before} training.

\paragraph{LLM Agent Environments and Reasoning.}
ScienceWorld \citep{scienceworld-2022} serves as our primary testbed; ALFWorld \citep{alfworld-2021} provides a complementary environment; AgentBench \citep{agentbench-2024} offers systematic evaluation. ReAct \citep{react-2023} serves as our internal baseline; Reflexion \citep{reflexion-2023} extends this with verbal self-reflection; LATS \citep{lats-2024} adds tree search. The concurrent SALT~\citep{salt-2026} assigns step-level credit on ALFWorld, complementing our episode-level decomposition.

\paragraph{Safe RL and Causal Grounding.}
Death avoidance connects to constrained MDPs \citep{altman1999constrained}, risk-sensitive RL \citep{mihatsch2002risk}, safe RL \citep{garcia2015comprehensive,achiam2017cpo,moldovan2012safe}, and shielding \citep{alshiekh2018safe}; however, classical methods assume known dynamics or differentiable constraints, unavailable for LLM agents. \citet{ruan2024failures} identify catastrophic actions as a failure category; SafeRoute \citep{saferoute-2025} and Agent-SafetyBench \citep{agent-safetybench-2024} address model selection and systematic evaluation for agent safety. Our pure-strategy comparison is a simplified instance of the potential outcomes framework~\citep{rubin1974estimating}, eliminating confounding without structural causal models~\citep{pearl2009causality}. \citet{bonagiri2025check} show selective quitting yields safety gains; our framework explains \emph{why}---death avoidance dominates routing signal. The grounding failures underlying death events connect to hallucination detection~\citep{manakul2023selfcheckgpt,kadavath2022language}, where incorrect state beliefs produce catastrophic outcomes.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Signal granularity and router failure.}
The router diagnostic (\S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}) reveals a structural mismatch: death avoidance is a task-type-level binary property, but step-level features correlate weakly because death risk depends on multi-step trajectories. Six router variants converge on near-chance accuracy ($56$--$59\%$), consistent with signal limitation, though extreme class imbalance (3.7\% minority class) and train--deploy distribution shift~\citep{dagger-2011} make a data-limited explanation equally plausible. Two consequences were not predictable a priori: $10{\times}$ scaling to 72B fails to eliminate death dominance (DAF${}=92.4\%$, 5 internal-preferred vs.\ 9 at 7B), and Reflexion yields DAF${}>100\%$; cross-model DR stability ($36$--$43\%$) confirms death vulnerability is a task-environment property.

\paragraph{Oracle degeneracy and practical implications.}
Always-deterministic matches 96.3\% of oracle decisions (\S\ref{sec:baselines}), capping any router's marginal value at 2.89 points. Oracle access is the precondition for death avoidance, but $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = 2.84$ (9.6\% of total gap) shows the advantage concentrates almost entirely in death episodes rather than reflecting broad information privilege; ALFWorld (DAF${=}0\%$) confirms the framework diagnoses a qualitatively different mechanism under different oracle configurations. This motivates a practical heuristic: a ${\sim}$50-episode pilot computing label fraction determines whether always-deterministic suffices ($f_{\text{death}} > 75\%$) or router investment is warranted (Appendix~\ref{app:decision-framework}). For ScienceWorld specifically, action-space filtering reduces death rate by 19~pp (Appendix~\ref{app:grounding-ablation}). While all observed deaths stem from a single action pattern (\texttt{focus on}; Appendix~\ref{app:failure-modes}), the decomposition applies to any environment with asymmetric fatal outcomes. More generally, death avoidance dominates routing signal when three conditions hold: (i)~the environment contains binary catastrophic failures, (ii)~strategies exhibit asymmetric failure rates, and (iii)~the penalty for failure substantially exceeds non-death score differences. ScienceWorld satisfies all three ($P{=}100 \gg \Delta_\text{nd}{=}2.84$); MATH and ALFWorld lack condition~(i), explaining why the framework diagnoses qualitatively different mechanisms.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Signal granularity and router failure.}
The router diagnostic (\S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}) reveals a structural mismatch: death avoidance is a task-type-level binary property, but step-level features correlate weakly because death risk depends on multi-step trajectories. Six router variants converge on near-chance accuracy ($56$--$59\%$), consistent with signal limitation, though extreme class imbalance (3.7\% minority class) and train--deploy distribution shift~\citep{dagger-2011} make a data-limited explanation equally plausible. Two consequences were not predictable a priori: $10{\times}$ scaling to 72B fails to eliminate death dominance (DAF${}=92.4\%$, 5 internal-preferred vs.\ 9 at 7B), and Reflexion yields DAF${}>100\%$; cross-model DR stability ($36$--$43\%$) confirms death vulnerability is a task-environment property.

\paragraph{Oracle degeneracy and practical implications.}
Always-deterministic matches 96.3\% of oracle decisions (\S\ref{sec:baselines}), capping any router's marginal value at 2.89 points. Oracle access is the precondition for death avoidance, but $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = 2.84$ (9.6\% of total gap) shows the advantage concentrates almost entirely in death episodes rather than reflecting broad information privilege; ALFWorld (DAF${=}0\%$) confirms the framework diagnoses a qualitatively different mechanism under different oracle configurations. This motivates a practical heuristic: a ${\sim}$50-episode pilot computing label fraction determines whether always-deterministic suffices ($f_{\text{death}} > 75\%$) or router investment is warranted (Appendix~\ref{app:decision-framework}). For ScienceWorld specifically, action-space filtering reduces death rate by 19~pp (Appendix~\ref{app:grounding-ablation}). While all observed deaths stem from a single action pattern (\texttt{focus on}; Appendix~\ref{app:failure-modes}), the decomposition applies to any environment where three conditions hold: binary catastrophic failures, asymmetric failure rates across strategies, and $P \gg \Delta_\text{nd}$. ScienceWorld satisfies all three; MATH and ALFWorld lack the first, explaining the different diagnoses.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/pipeline_overview.pdf}
\caption{Counterfactual episode collection and analysis pipeline. Each task instance is run under both pure strategies independently, producing episode-level labels via Algorithm~\ref{alg:labeling}.}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure}

Our diagnostic framework quantifies what drives routing signal in interactive environments through two components: (i)~episode-level counterfactual labeling that generates routing preferences from paired pure-strategy outcomes (\S\ref{sec:collection}--\ref{sec:labeling}); and (ii)~the Death Avoidance Factor (DAF), which decomposes the routing score gap into death-avoidance and non-death components (\S\ref{sec:daf}). A supplementary Signal Concentration Index (SCI; Appendix~\ref{app:sci}) measures whether signal is concentrated in a few task types. Episode-level bootstrap resampling provides 95\% confidence intervals for all metrics.

\subsection{Problem Setup}
\label{sec:problem-setup}

Consider an interactive environment with observation space $\mathcal{O}$ and action space $\mathcal{A}$. An agent policy $\pi: \mathcal{O}^* \rightarrow \mathcal{A}$ maps observation histories to actions. We define two computation modes $\mathcal{M} = \{{\tt int}, {\tt det}\}$: \textbf{internal} (${\tt int}$), where the agent predicts intermediate observations via chain-of-thought reasoning (ReAct~\citep{react-2023}), with prediction errors compounding across steps; and \textbf{deterministic} (${\tt det}$), a privileged-information strategy (an umbrella term: ground-truth simulator access in ScienceWorld, code interpreter in MATH, admissible-action enumeration in ALFWorld---see \S\ref{sec:cross-env} for environment-specific operationalizations) that eliminates observation uncertainty; these qualitative differences affect what routing signals the framework can detect. A \emph{routing policy} $\rho: \mathcal{O}^* \rightarrow \mathcal{M}$ selects which computation mode to use at each step; our analysis investigates what signal is available to learn $\rho$.

\subsection{Counterfactual Episode Collection}
\label{sec:collection}

Per-step oracle labeling assigns routing labels by comparing single-step outcomes under each computation mode. Under sparse terminal rewards, this approach degenerates: on ScienceWorld \citep{scienceworld-2022}, 97.4\% of steps produce identical intermediate scores regardless of computation mode (5{,}935 of 6{,}094 steps across 11 prescreened task types), yielding no per-step routing signal. The 97.4\% tie rate arises because ScienceWorld assigns rewards only at episode termination; intermediate steps update environment state but do not change the score, so a per-step oracle can only distinguish strategies through downstream consequences visible at episode end. Episode-level comparison is therefore the principled granularity for sparse-reward environments, not a fallback from per-step analysis: it directly measures whether one strategy produces better terminal outcomes than the other, bypassing the uninformative intermediate steps entirely.

\paragraph{Pure-strategy trajectories.}
For a task instance $t$ and computation mode $m \in \mathcal{M}$, a \emph{pure-strategy trajectory} is the complete episode obtained by applying mode $m$ at every step:
\begin{equation}
    \tau_m^{(t)} = (o_1, a_1, o_2, a_2, \ldots, o_T, a_T)
\end{equation}
where $T \leq T_{\max}$ is the episode length (capped at $T_{\max} = 30$ steps). Both trajectories $\tau_{\tt int}^{(t)}$ and $\tau_{\tt det}^{(t)}$ are executed under identical initial conditions (same random seed, same task variation), so performance differences arise solely from the computation mode choice.

Each trajectory $\tau$ yields a terminal score $s(\tau) \in \mathbb{Z}$ and a death indicator $d(\tau) \in \{0, 1\}$. When the agent dies ($d = 1$), the environment imposes a penalty: $s(\tau) = -P$ where $P > 0$ ($P = 100$ in ScienceWorld). The episode pair produces a tuple:
\begin{multline}
    \bigl(s^{\tt int}, s^{\tt det}, d^{\tt int}, d^{\tt det}\bigr) \triangleq \\
    \bigl(s(\tau_{\tt int}),\, s(\tau_{\tt det}),\, d(\tau_{\tt int}),\, d(\tau_{\tt det})\bigr)
\end{multline}

We use Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct~\citep{qwen25-2024} (vLLM v0.21.0) on ScienceWorld \citep{scienceworld-2022}, a text-based environment spanning 30 task types with a $-100$ death penalty. After prescreening, we collect 245 episode pairs across 11 task types (20--40 per type). The framework is applicable to any environment where paired episodes under two computation modes can be collected with binary success/failure indicators. We apply it unchanged to ALFWorld and MATH in \S\ref{sec:math-contrast}--\ref{sec:cross-env}.

\subsection{Episode-Level Labeling}
\label{sec:labeling}

We define a labeling function $L$ that maps a counterfactual pair to a routing preference:
\begin{multline}
    L\!\bigl(\tau_{\tt int}^{(t)}, \tau_{\tt det}^{(t)}\bigr) \;\rightarrow \\
    \ell \in \{{\tt det\text{-}pref},\; {\tt int\text{-}pref},\; {\tt no\text{-}pref}\}
\end{multline}

The labeling function is \emph{death-aware}: it prioritizes survival over score, then compares scores among same-status episodes (Algorithm~\ref{alg:labeling}; Appendix~\ref{app:labeling}). Both-dead episodes are assigned \texttt{no\_preference} because ScienceWorld's death penalty is fixed ($-100$) regardless of timing, so death order carries no routing signal. ScienceWorld scores are integer-valued, so we use exact (in)equality with no threshold. Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline} illustrates the overall pipeline; label distributions are reported in \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}.

\subsection{Death Avoidance Factor}
\label{sec:daf}

To quantify the contribution of death avoidance to routing signal, we decompose the mean score gap between strategies. Let $\delta = \mathbb{E}[d^{\tt int}] - \mathbb{E}[d^{\tt det}]$ denote the death-rate differential and $\Delta_{\text{nd}}$ the mean score gap restricted to episodes where neither strategy dies. Partitioning episodes by death status, death-asymmetric episodes (where only one strategy dies) contribute a per-episode gap of $P + \bar{s}_{\text{surv}}$, where $\bar{s}_{\text{surv}}$ is the surviving strategy's mean score; when $\bar{s}_{\text{surv}} \ll P$ (in our data, $\bar{s}_{\text{surv}} = 0.87$, or $0.9\%$ of $P$), these episodes contribute ${\approx}\,\delta P$ in aggregate. The full gap thus decomposes as:
\begin{equation}
    \Delta_{\text{full}} \approx \underbrace{\delta \cdot P}_{\text{death contribution}} + \Delta_{\text{nd}}
\end{equation}
The approximation residual is $29.60 - 29.37 = 0.23$ ($0.8\%$ of $\Delta_{\text{full}}$).

We define the Death Avoidance Factor (DAF) as the fraction of the full score gap attributable to death avoidance:
\begin{equation}
    \text{DAF} = 1 - \frac{\Delta_{\text{nd}}}{\Delta_{\text{full}}} \approx \frac{\delta P}{\delta P + \Delta_{\text{nd}}}
\end{equation}%
\footnote{When $\Delta_{\text{nd}} < 0$ (deterministic strategy scores lower than internal on non-death episodes), DAF exceeds 1; death avoidance then overcompensates for a non-death disadvantage.}

Since DAF $= \delta P / (\delta P + \Delta_{\text{nd}})$, it increases monotonically with $P$, a mathematical consequence rather than an empirical finding. For context: DAF $= 50.4\%$ at $P{=}10$, $82.6\%$ at $P{=}50$, and $90.4\%$ at $P{=}100$ (full curve in Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}). We treat the label fraction (78.9\%) as the primary, penalty-invariant diagnostic and DAF as a supplementary, environment-specific measure. In ScienceWorld, $P$ affects only post-hoc score calculation; the agent's policy (ReAct prompting) does not include $P$ in its observation, so death rates and $\Delta_{\text{nd}}$ are $P$-invariant by design~\citep{ng1999policy} (Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}).

\paragraph{Relationship between DAF and label fraction.}
DAF (90.4\%) exceeds the label fraction (78.9\%) because death episodes carry disproportionately large score gaps ($P{=}100 \gg$ typical non-death $\Delta{\approx}29$), amplifying their weight in score-weighted aggregation. The label fraction, invariant to penalty magnitude, serves as the primary finding; DAF reflects the score-space reality under ScienceWorld's reward structure.

\paragraph{Statistical inference.}
We quantify uncertainty via episode-level bootstrap resampling ($B = 10{,}000$). Each resample draws $N = 245$ episodes with replacement, recomputes all metrics, and derives percentile-based 95\% CIs. We resample at the episode level (not the step level) because the episode is the unit of counterfactual comparison. Penalty sensitivity analysis across hypothetical $P$ values is in Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}.

We also measure whether death-avoidance signal is concentrated in a few task types via a Signal Concentration Index (SCI $\in [0, 1]$; Appendix~\ref{app:sci}), reported in \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}.

\paragraph{Scope of episode-level analysis.}
At step level, 97.4\% of oracle comparisons yield ties (\S\ref{sec:collection}); of 2{,}040 training steps, only 0.3\% exhibit strict directional conflict with episode labels, with 60.3\% pre-event neutral (neither strategy advantaged). Episode labels thus apply correct direction to uninformative steps---a granularity mismatch, not label error---explaining why step-level router training approaches chance performance (\S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}).

\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Tool Use and External Computation in LLMs.}
A growing body of work augments language models with external tools \citep{tool-learning-survey-2024}, spanning several functional categories.
Self-supervised methods learn tool invocation from loss signals \citep{toolformer-2023,toolace-2025}, token-level methods embed tools in the vocabulary \citep{toolkengpt-2023}, task-level decomposition routes subtasks to specialized modules \citep{art-2023,chameleon-2023}, and RL-based methods learn routing from reward \citep{retool-2025}.
All focus on \emph{learning to use tools}; we ask a prior question: \emph{what signal drives the routing decision?}
Concurrent work examines \emph{when} to invoke tools: tool-call decisions correlate with uncertainty~\citep{tool-overuse-illusion-2026}, are encoded in hidden states~\citep{when2tool-2026}, impose a ``tool-use tax''~\citep{tool-use-tax-2026}, or follow necessity-utility-affordability logic~\citep{to-call-or-not-2026}. Our decomposition yields a concrete answer: 78.9\% of non-tie routing labels trace to catastrophic-failure prevention.

\paragraph{Adaptive Computation and Routing.}
Adaptive routing traces back to learned halting policies \citep{graves2016act}. Mixture-of-Recursions \citep{mixture-of-recursions-2025} learns token-level recursion depths in the LLM era; concurrent work further diversifies routing granularity: CogRouter \citep{cogrouter-2026} adapts per-step depth, ARES \citep{ares-2026} selects effort levels, and MTRouter \citep{mtrouter-2026} addresses multi-turn settings; in death-dominated environments, the task-type-level binary signal is structurally inaccessible to these step-level approaches (\S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}).
RouteLLM \citep{routellm-2024} learns routers from preference data to select between strong and weak models; cost-optimization routing (FrugalGPT~\citep{frugalgpt-2023}; AutoMix~\citep{automix-2024}) cascades queries across model tiers. These methods optimize \emph{which} model to invoke; our framework diagnoses \emph{what} drives the routing decision.
Concurrently, \citet{budget-aware-routing-2026} train episode-level routing policies under sparse terminal rewards, the closest setting to ours.
Unlike token-level adaptive computation (Mixture-of-Depths~\citep{mixture-of-depths-2024}; speculative decoding~\citep{leviathan2023speculative}; early exit~\citep{xin2020deebert,zhou2020bert}) and Mixture-of-Experts architectures~\citep{switch-transformer-2022,gshard-2021}, our framework operates at episode-level granularity, diagnosing aggregate routing signal composition rather than optimizing per-token compute allocation. Counterfactual data augmentation~\citep{kaushik2020learning} edits inputs to flip labels; our framework executes full counterfactual \emph{episodes} to decompose routing signal at the environment level.

\paragraph{Counterfactual Evaluation in RL.}
Prior work generates counterfactual explanations by perturbing individual actions \citep{olson2021counterfactual, gajcin2023counterfactual, amitai2024explaining}; recent and concurrent extensions target LLM agents~\citep{pona2025abstract,axis-2026,cso-2026}. We instead execute complete episodes under two strategies to decompose routing signal \emph{before} training.

\paragraph{LLM Agent Environments and Reasoning.}
ScienceWorld \citep{scienceworld-2022} serves as our primary testbed; ALFWorld \citep{alfworld-2021} provides a complementary environment; AgentBench \citep{agentbench-2024} offers systematic evaluation. ReAct \citep{react-2023} serves as our internal baseline; Reflexion \citep{reflexion-2023} extends this with verbal self-reflection; LATS \citep{lats-2024} adds tree search. The concurrent SALT~\citep{salt-2026} assigns step-level credit on ALFWorld, complementing our episode-level decomposition.

\paragraph{Safe RL and Causal Grounding.}
Death avoidance connects to constrained MDPs \citep{altman1999constrained}, risk-sensitive RL \citep{mihatsch2002risk}, safe RL \citep{garcia2015comprehensive,achiam2017cpo,moldovan2012safe}, and shielding \citep{alshiekh2018safe}; risk-sensitive approaches optimize for worst-case trajectory outcomes via CVaR~\citep{chow2015risk,tamar2015optimizing}, addressing a related but distinct problem: our framework diagnoses which routing signals derive from catastrophic risk rather than optimizing against it. Classical methods assume known dynamics or differentiable constraints, unavailable for LLM agents. \citet{ruan2024failures} identify catastrophic actions as a failure category; SafeRoute \citep{saferoute-2025} and Agent-SafetyBench \citep{agent-safetybench-2024} address model selection and systematic evaluation for agent safety. Our pure-strategy comparison is a simplified instance of the potential outcomes framework~\citep{rubin1974estimating}, eliminating confounding without structural causal models~\citep{pearl2009causality}. \citet{bonagiri2025check} show selective quitting yields safety gains; our framework explains \emph{why}---death avoidance dominates routing signal. The grounding failures underlying death events connect to hallucination detection~\citep{manakul2023selfcheckgpt,kadavath2022language}, where incorrect state beliefs produce catastrophic outcomes.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Signal granularity and router failure.}
The router diagnostic (\S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}) reveals a structural mismatch: death avoidance is a task-type-level binary property, but step-level features correlate weakly because death risk depends on multi-step trajectories. Six router variants converge on near-chance accuracy ($56$--$59\%$), consistent with signal limitation, though extreme class imbalance (3.7\% minority class) and train--deploy distribution shift~\citep{dagger-2011} make a data-limited explanation equally plausible. Two consequences were not predictable a priori: $10{\times}$ scaling to 72B fails to eliminate death dominance (DAF${}=92.4\%$, 5 internal-preferred vs.\ 9 at 7B), and Reflexion yields DAF${}>100\%$; cross-model DR stability ($36$--$43\%$) confirms death vulnerability is a task-environment property.

\paragraph{Oracle degeneracy and practical implications.}
Always-deterministic matches 96.3\% of oracle decisions (\S\ref{sec:baselines}), capping any router's marginal value at 2.89 points. Oracle access is the precondition for death avoidance, but $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = 2.84$ (9.6\% of total gap) shows the advantage concentrates almost entirely in death episodes rather than reflecting broad information privilege; ALFWorld (DAF${=}0\%$) confirms the framework diagnoses a qualitatively different mechanism under different oracle configurations. A ${\sim}$50-episode pilot computing label fraction determines whether always-deterministic suffices (Appendix~\ref{app:decision-framework}). Action-space filtering reduces death rate by 19~pp (Appendix~\ref{app:grounding-ablation}). While all observed deaths stem from a single action pattern (\texttt{focus on}; Appendix~\ref{app:failure-modes}), the decomposition applies to any environment where three conditions hold: binary catastrophic failures, asymmetric failure rates across strategies, and $P \gg \Delta_\text{nd}$. ScienceWorld satisfies all three; MATH and ALFWorld lack the first, explaining the different diagnoses.

